From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 06:03:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 100/185] segacd: the log buffer takes B:'s eleven data sectors

3584 was inherited from the older graft and the round is bigger now --
about 305 bytes, so eleven rounds. B: is sixteen 512-byte sectors,
five spent on the reserved sector, two FATs and a 32-entry root, which
leaves 5632 and caps any file there whatever the buffer holds.
Eighteen rounds for two kilobytes of BSS.

Not urgent. Eleven rounds of an intermittent fault is a usable sample
and the disc already burned is unaffected.
---
 bios/bios.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/bios.c b/bios/bios.c
index c26fd5c..312ca10 100644
--- a/bios/bios.c
+++ b/bios/bios.c
@@ -824,8 +824,17 @@ BOOL can_shutdown(void)
  * flash cart writes it to an SD card.
  *
  * When the buffer fills it stops: the file stops changing and says so,
- * and the screen keeps running. */
-#define CDLOG_MAX 3584
+ * and the screen keeps running.
+ *
+ * The size is everything B: can hold. That volume is sixteen 512-byte
+ * sectors, one cluster each, of which five go to the reserved sector,
+ * two FATs and a 32-entry root -- so eleven are left and a file cannot
+ * exceed 5632 bytes however much RAM this buffer has. A round costs
+ * about 305 bytes, which is eighteen rounds. The failures being chased
+ * here are intermittent and six rounds was already the most useful
+ * sample this port has had; there is no reason to leave the other
+ * seven on the floor. */
+#define CDLOG_MAX 5632
 
 static char cdlog[CDLOG_MAX];
 static UWORD cdlog_len;
